{"name":"Ice Blue","hex":"#739bd0","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ice blue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very pale blue colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a very pale blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice_blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shades of blue","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color blue may differ in hue, chroma, or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a blue or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Shades_of_Blue.svg/330px-Shades_of_Blue.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_blue","wikidata":"Q7460342"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/500px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/1280px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png/500px-Twenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwenty_hues_of_the_Munsell_color_system_at_maximum_chroma.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg/500px-A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg/1280px-A_Texas_bluebonnet_at_late_afternoon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u